  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The spec is a clean, transport-agnostic composition shell: opaque payload plus pCID dispatch avoids hard-coding UDP details. But unsigned v0 delegates authorship, integrity, and most session evidence elsewhere, so it only partially demonstrates a sufficient next layer above UDP-feed.",
    "strengths": [
      "Strong boundary clarity and explicit failure at dispatch: payload stays opaque until the named pCID handler runs, and unknown handlers return unsupported-pCID instead of speculative parsing.",
      "Unknown-pCID store and forward supports sparse knowledge and forward compatibility without a central registry.",
      "Deterministic CBOR over a minimal two-slot envelope is simple and plausible to implement."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No built-in signature or authorship record, so auditability and peer-local accountability are weak.",
      "Ordering, relay evidence, and session semantics are pushed to wrappers or payload protocols rather than specified here.",
      "The specimen is still draft and unfrozen, with long-term pCID and extension practice not yet settled."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "If integrity or authorship come from the transport in practice, the next layer may need transport-specific knowledge, leaking the binding details this scenario tries to avoid.",
      "Unsigned envelopes are exposed to spoofing, replay, or tampering unless an outer or inner layer compensates."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What exact next-layer group/session contract above UDP-feed carries ordering, membership, and replay protection when this envelope is used?",
      "Would a signed sibling envelope preserve the same boundary clarity with better auditability and lower risk?",
      "How will positional-array extension work without breaking existing handlers, and when would a new pCID be required?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
